  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nCurrently, a strong upper high is centered across the desert \nsouthwest with a shortwave and downstream jet approaching the Great \nLakes. Bermuda High pressure is centered in the Atlantic. This \ninitial setup has put us in a broad southwesterly flow pattern. \nSeasonally warm and humid conditions continue with sufficient \nmoisture for diurnal showers and storms, already ongoing along the \nhigher terrain. With 1,500 J/kg or higher MLCAPE and no shear, a few \nstronger storms may be possible, but the overall severe threat is \nlimited by lapse rates of 5.5 C/km or less. Isolated flooding also \nremains possible for slow-moving or repeated storms. For Friday, the \nshortwave advances towards the area, but the upper jet will weaken \nand lift north. The overall environment tomorrow looks comparable to \ntoday with slightly higher coverage possible due to the advancing \nshortwave. By Saturday, the shortwave will advance even further into \nthe area, leading to upper-level height falls. Based on the current \ndata, the resulting convective environment looks more favorable than \ntoday and tomorrow with mid-level lapse rates reaching near 6 C/km \nand MLCAPE exceeding 2,000 J/kg. PWATs will remain in the 1.7 to 1.9 \ninch range. This may provide even better coverage and slightly \nhigher potential for strong to severe storms. \n\nBy Sunday, upper-level flow shifts northward with variable \nindications of a 500mb shortwave. Overall, there is a signal for \nless moisture, which would limit coverage compared to previous days. \nThis pattern will be similar on Monday as well. By Tuesday and \nWednesday, a combination of low-level heating and cooling aloft due \nto shortwaves will produce a more notable convective environment. \nCurrent indications suggest lapse rates reaching 6 C/km to even 7 \nC/km, which would provide greater instability. A lot is uncertain \nabout the exact placement of this environment, but strong to severe \nstorms with damaging winds are certainly possible.",
